** Opponent's 20,000 + my forced blind (likely 100 chips based on chip count)\n\n**Hand Strength Analysis:**\n- KJ offsuit is a medium-strength hand - it's above average but not premium\n- Against a random hand, KJ offsuit has approximately 60% equity\n- However, if opponent is all-in pre-flop on the very first hand, they likely have a strong hand (pairs AA-TT, or strong aces like AK, AQ)\n- Against a tight range of premium hands, KJ offsuit performs poorly (roughly 30-40% win rate)\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n1. This is hand 1 of 20 - very early in the game\n2. Calling would risk essentially my entire stack (19,900 of my 20,000)\n3. If I lose, I'd be severely crippled for the remaining 19 hands\n4. If opponent has a premium pair (TT+) or AK/AQ, I'm an underdog\n5. The aggressive all-in on hand 1 suggests a strong holding\n6. Even if I win this hand, the risk-reward isn't favorable given it's so early\n\n**Math:** \n- I need to call 19,900 to win approximately 40,000 total pot\n- Pot odds: ~2:1, meaning I need ~33% equity\n- However, against a reasonable all-in range, KJ offsuit likely has 35-45% equity at best\n- The marginal positive expectation doesn't justify risking my entire tournament on hand 1\n\nReason: While KJ offsuit is a decent hand, facing an all-in bet on the very first hand of a 20-hand match is too risky. The opponent's aggressive action likely indicates a premium hand where I'm an underdog. With 19 hands remaining, preserving my stack and waiting for better spots is the superior strategy. Busting on hand 1 would leave me with almost no chips for the rest of the match.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
